WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.940
SPEAKER 1: Google
Cloud Next 2017

00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:04.940
hosted an almost disturbingly
large number of talks

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.462
detailing the future of Cloud.

00:00:06.462 --> 00:00:08.420
If you didn't get a chance
to catch the session

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:11.970
called Stackdriver: Monitor,
Debug, Fix, then stay tuned,

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:13.470
because here is the recap.

00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:17.390
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:21.010
SPEAKER 1: Stackdriver is a
SAS package for monitoring,

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:23.880
logging, and diagnostics
that help you gain insight

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.700
into applications that run
on Google Cloud Platform

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:28.530
and Amazon Web Services.

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:30.480
SPEAKER 2: One big
advantage of Stackdriver

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:33.210
is not having to bounce between
several different web sites

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:35.460
to look at your error
round-up, aggregated logs,

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:37.136
and application
performance monitoring.

00:00:37.136 --> 00:00:38.760
SPEAKER 1: To understand
more about how

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:40.770
to use this great tool,
let's take a deeper

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:42.030
look at monitoring.

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:44.160
Every application needs
some sort of monitoring.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:45.940
And nearly every
application needs

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:47.970
some sort of uptime check.

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:49.590
After that, we want
to monitor things

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:51.210
that are actionable for you.

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:54.300
A good monitor for web app
is the response code ratios.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:57.386
If I'm getting a certain
number of 400 or 500 responses,

00:00:57.386 --> 00:00:59.010
that's a problem I
want to be aware of.

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:01.051
SPEAKER 2: You also want
to monitor your database

00:01:01.051 --> 00:01:02.316
to make sure it's keeping up.

00:01:02.316 --> 00:01:04.440
One of the cool things
about Stackdriver monitoring

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.660
is that it can detect
what you're likely

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:09.480
running on your VM and support
several common third party

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:13.590
tools, including MySQL, Apache,
Cassandra, CouchDB, MongoDB,

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:15.720
Postgres, and many, many more.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:18.390
For supported services, the
Stackdriver monitoring agent

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:20.400
will discover what
you're running

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:23.460
even if you haven't configured
the server-specific plugin.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:25.350
It does this by
examining instance names

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:28.500
and looking for specific ports
being opened in firewall roles.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:30.960
SPEAKER 1: If it finds a
supported third party tool,

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:33.210
it will populate it
in the resources list

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:34.560
to include that service.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:37.166
There's also metric supported
for many Google services.

00:01:37.166 --> 00:01:38.790
For example, you can
monitor the number

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:40.770
of interceptions that
the App Engine does

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:43.470
to prevent DOS attacks, or the
number of operations happening

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:45.630
in a Firebase database,
and the uploaded

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:47.250
bites per minute to BitQuery.

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:49.530
SPEAKER 2: And finally,
most production apps

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:53.120
need some sort of crash or
error reporting and loading.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.960
Stackdriver has an error
reporting tool built in,

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:57.960
so you can subscribe to
alerts and get notified

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:00.360
about new crashes with
automatic throttle back,

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:03.240
so you aren't getting nagged
over and over if everything

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:04.974
is going horribly wrong.

00:02:04.974 --> 00:02:06.390
There are several
alerting methods

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:09.539
available, including Slack,
Hipchat, pager, SMS, email,

00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:10.620
and the app.

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:12.930
And you can include a custom
message with the alert,

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:14.670
perhaps explaining
what debugging steps

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:16.265
to take if the
alert is triggered.

00:02:16.265 --> 00:02:17.640
SPEAKER 1: Once
you get an alert,

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.760
you can dig into it using
Stackdriver Error Reporting.

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:23.989
Here is the Error Reporting
dashboard showing some crashes.

00:02:23.989 --> 00:02:26.280
You can drill down to see
how many times this error has

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:28.350
happened and, for
App Engine apps,

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:30.270
which version it
first happened in

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:33.240
and which version
it last happened in.

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:35.130
Like monitoring,
an error analysis

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:37.440
works in a hybrid
cloud environment.

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:39.090
There are
language-specific libraries

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:40.914
for the seven main
Google Cloud languages

00:02:40.914 --> 00:02:42.330
if you wish to
send your own crash

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:44.130
reports to Error Reporting.

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:45.930
And if you set up
Stackdriver logging,

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:47.190
you get Error Reporting.

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:49.290
SPEAKER 2: Looking a
bit deeper, this error,

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:51.060
you can see the
stack trace, which

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:53.940
offers an idea of where
something may have gone wrong.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:56.310
The log lets us see more
information about the error,

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:59.652
like the request parameters
and the HTTP response code.

00:02:59.652 --> 00:03:01.110
Using the Stackdriver
debugger, you

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:02.940
can figure out what
the application

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:05.370
state is at that point.

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:07.920
The debugger lets you get a
snapshot of the application

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.740
state at a given point
during execution.

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:14.190
The runtime debugger currently
supports Java, Python, and Go

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:17.197
applications running on App
Engine or Compute Engine.

00:03:17.197 --> 00:03:19.530
SPEAKER 1: Now, if you want
to see Stackdriver in action

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:21.780
with a series of
awesome demos, check out

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:23.970
the full video on YouTube.

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:27.310
And if you want more recaps
on great Next content,

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:29.320
make sure to check out
the rest of our playlist.

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:32.280
And don't forget, the
Google Cloud Summit,

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.110
coming soon to a city near you.

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:36.260
[MUSIC PLAYING]

